  "solution": {
    "js": "function biggestPond(posts) {\n  let left = 0\n  let right = posts.length - 1\n  let best = 0\n  while (left < right) {\n    const height = Math.min(posts[left], posts[right])\n    best = Math.max(best, height * (right - left))\n    if (posts[left] < posts[right]) left++\n    else right--\n  }\n  return best\n}\n",
    "py": "def biggest_pond(posts):\n    left, right = 0, len(posts) - 1\n    best = 0\n    while left < right:\n        height = min(posts[left], posts[right])\n        best = max(best, height * (right - left))\n        if posts[left] < posts[right]:\n            left += 1\n        else:\n            right -= 1\n    return best\n",
    "explanation": "Any pair using the shorter end post and some inner post is narrower and capped by the same short height, so none beats the current area. Discarding the shorter post is therefore safe, and the scan visits each post once."
